Date: Sat, 4 Dec 1993 12:26:46 -0500
From: pmetzger@lehman.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: The Decline and Fall of the Extropians List

Tim notes that I haven't been posting. Its largely his fault. I had a
long phone conversation with him about Life, the Universe, and
Everything, in which I realized that I was no longer on track -- I was
spending too much of my time talking about things and not enough doing
them. I decided to reevaluate EVERYTHING I spent time on.

I looked at the Extropians list, realized that 90% of the time I spent
on it was wasted, and I put it into digest mode while I've been
deciding whether to dump it completely. I've found the recent
discussions on mind altering substances and business both mildly
interesting, so I've kept digest mode, for now, but I'm not replying
to anything, and I've deliberately crippled my mail environment to
make it hard for me to reply to things in case I get tempted.

Its quite natural, by the way, that the list has decayed to
uselessness -- after a while, the interesting topics have been
legitimately discussed, and there is a limit to how much people want
to talk about them, and if the same people stay around that leaves
just chaff. 

The Extropians list is much like a cocktail party that was thrilling
in its first hours, so the guests have decided to stay for a week. The
night is okay, even though the guests have nothing to give them
nutrition but peanuts and alchohol, but after a while people start
getting hungry, the conversation starts getting thin, and people start
getting tired. Its not that the topics aren't of interest, but for
anyone with a real life there is only so much time available to obsess
about politics and philosophy, and then you want to get back to your
life until the next party. I feel the list is in much the same
position.

Perry

