I can't speak for anyone but myself (to repeat an (ideally unnecessary)
tautology), but I have been enjoying the list lately just as much as ever;
perhaps even more so, now that the flames have died down and the only thing
I'm excluding is the nightly HeX market report. Specifically, Kennita's
presence and participation have been a blessing, at least as far as *I'm*
concerned. (Take heart, K -- not all [O|o]bjectivists are anal-retentive,
habitually depressed control freaks. My parents managed to raise me quite
nicely without ever specifically attempting to preach or drum philosophy into
my head; I simply absorbed it, by osmosis, from their everyday words and
actions. When I read my mom's copy of Atlas Shrugged at age 12, it was merely
a confirmation of everything I already "knew". I'd be more than happy to
further discuss childhood, child-rearing, etc., via private mail.)
 
Though I have to wonder at Tim's (seemingly) implied assumption, namely, that
it's necessary to post to the list to derive value from it. Logically, it
would seem that one derives value from the list by reading, comprehending and
appreciating the posts contained therein (and perhaps by putting some of the
practical advice to use, such as the recent excellent threads on investing and
entrepreneurship). I can unequivocally state that, by this definition, I have
derived great value from the list; I find it one of the highest quality lists
I have ever subscribed to, and an excellent example of the principle that the
fewer people involved, the better the S/N ratio. I have already made room in
my budget for the twelve FRN's it will require to keep me on the list
throughout 1994, and consider it well-spent.
 
By POSTING to the list, one (hopefully) CONTRIBUTES value to the list.
 
Not being a "barrel-chested, cleft-chinned" intellectual type on the level of
Tim, Nick or Jamie, I rarely contribute to the "hard" threads, mainly because
I'm smart enough to know my limitations. I don't speak unless I genuinely have
something to say and feel it will be worthwhile -- a practice I'm sure many of
you wish that more people would follow.
 
I sincerely hope that lurking will not become a designated List offense.
"Mandatory posting minimums" would be a real bitch. :)
 


