Date: Sat, 4 Dec 1993 13:38:24 -0800
From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Subject: Love and Relationship in the Transhuman Age

"The more you love, the more you _can_ love -- and the more intensely
you love.  Nor is there any limit on how _many_ you can love.  If a
person had time enough, he could love all of that majority who are
decent and just."
     -- from the Notebooks of Lazarus Long in _Time Enough for Love_

Do other Extropians believe this?  I do.  Without getting into a
debate on what "love" means, I'll just say that I haven't thought of
any more adequate term to describe what I feel for many wonderful
people I know, including many people on this list.  I have often felt
ready to burst with that feeling, and only since I've discovered
Extropianism have I contemplated the possibility of expanding myself
to hold it.

Would a "Jupiter-sized" brain actually have a greater capacity to
love?  Would love seem unimportant or irrelevant at that stage?  Would
romantic love still involve two, or would it also expand, to encompass
ten or ten million?  Would it prove easier or harder to find?  Galaxies
wheel awfully far apart.

What role will physical affection play in the transhuman age?  (I
don't just refer to sex here, though sex certainly plays a part -- I
mean all the touches people exchange affectionately:  hugs, kisses,
handshakes, pats on the back, consolatory strokes, running fingers
through hair, etc.).  Will we find value in still having bodies that
want touching?  If so, will we actually get together to do that
touching, or will we rely on VR?

To bring this back to the nearer-term:  what effect will conducting
more and more of our communication via the net have on us emotionally?
As we make more and more friends whom we've never met, as we begin to
conduct personal business with people we not only have never met but
could never identify, as we anonymize ourselves and encrypt our
transactions to protect ourselves not only from outsiders but from
each other, will we find that we cannot get enough information about
people to bring our intuition to bear, and thus decide that that
intuition has no relevance and stop using it?

I get a lot of information about people from touching them, and I feel
more or less handicapped in situations where I can't touch people
(a lot of the time, these days).  Tangential to another topic:  I
thought of saying that I don't trust people I can't touch, but on
reflection I realize that that depends on what I want to trust them to
do.  Having managed to get myself into highly irrational and agitated
states without benefit of pharmaceuticals, should I get into such a
state under the influence of some, I would need to trust the person(s) 
with me to use appropriate touches (hugs, etc.) to calm me rather than
trying to "talk me down", which I have no reason to think would work
since it never has before.  So even people whom I might trust with my
life in an emergency, or with my money in a pinch, I might not trust
with my emotional well-being in such a situation, based on my
intuition of them in the context of touching.

So, what might the future hold for us touchy-feely types?  I look
forward to hearing what you have to say.

  +---- I'd put "Hugs" here, but I figured it would make too many people
  |     uncomfortable -- does that seem considerate or just cowardly?
  V
Cheers,
"The anti-Tim" :-)

