> From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
> Yes... but this is probably extremely difficult; even assuming that
> the change in brainwaves can be made immediately, to get a 50 millisecond
> recognition time, you'll have to be able to perform a systemic
> change in brainwave frequencies above 40 Hz or so (assuming two waves
> are needed for reliable recognition) -- is this possible? 

Certainly it is possible to make a change in mental state with the
necessary speed.  Whether we can register it is another question.
The EEG is really a pretty useless phenomenon -- not to knock those
working with it, but the reason they use it is that it's easy to
measure.  One would probably need a technique to register neural
activity that isn't large-scale synchronous, in order to get
sufficient speed.  This would also let you look elsewhere than the
visual cortex.  Neurobiologists are putting time and money into NMR
microscopy, which might do the trick.  I hope there's something
simpler.

Really, the eye tracker is the important part of the setup.  If
tapping a button rather than thinking will save you a few million on
the hardware, you'll probably be glad to tap a button.

