Date: Sat, 4 Dec 1993 17:15:50 -0600 (CST)
From: cman@caffeine.io.com (Douglas Barnes)
Subject: Love and Relationship in the Transhuman Age

I'm still processing some of your longer term questions here, but I'm
going to take exception to the following point:

> To bring this back to the nearer-term:  what effect will conducting
> more and more of our communication via the net have on us emotionally?
> As we make more and more friends whom we've never met, as we begin to
> conduct personal business with people we not only have never met but
> could never identify, as we anonymize ourselves and encrypt our
> transactions to protect ourselves not only from outsiders but from
> each other, will we find that we cannot get enough information about
> people to bring our intuition to bear, and thus decide that that
> intuition has no relevance and stop using it?
> 

I think that some of this protection will actually let people open
up more securely with one another, rather than having the "chilling
effect" you suggest. For instance, people involved in low-bandwidth
VR right now often take on personas that allow them to explore aspects
of their personalities that they don't want tracked back to their 
True Names or their physical selves.

What does it mean to know someone electronically? Can you say that you
know someone "better" electronically because you could theoretically
connect the artifacts of their online presence with their True Name and
physical selves? Clearly it does have interpersonal impact; relationships
done through "firewalls" that protect root identity are going to have 
somewhat inhibited potential in meat space, but are no more limited in 
scope within the electronic domain than relationships with people where 
you could theoretically hop on a plane and drive to their door. (Call 
first, btw :)

At IO, many stafers are currently struggling with their feelings towards
"cybersex," which has become somewhat the rage among people on our MOO.
Some think it's the neatest thing since sliced bread, others think it
is pathetic and sad, still others think it's immoral and perverted. I
say, "let a thousand flowers bloom," and leave it at that. In any event,
many people get something (a lot of something, judging from the time
they spend) from this activity, which speaks strongly for the power of
the brain as a sex organ...

I've gone over this territory in my head quite a bit, since the now-
infamous "Pseudospoofing" debacle on cypherpunks. LD was clearly
unhinged, but certainly stimulated some thought on the subject. 

Frankly, as far as an electronic relationship goes, it makes little
or no difference if I am responding to artifacts traceable to a Real
Person (tm) or a cleverly designed front. It's nice to know what 
Tim May looks like, so I have an image in my head while I'm reading
his posts, but ere long we're going to be adding more and more graphics
and whatever into the mix, and we'll all be able to project images to
one another whether our root identities are firewalled or not.

> I get a lot of information about people from touching them, and I feel
> more or less handicapped in situations where I can't touch people
> (a lot of the time, these days).  

I mentioned earlier that people are going to be able to project graphic 
images of themselves; actually, this is old hat in some contexts. For 
instance, Sierra Online allows you to construct an image of yourself 
using a simplfied kit similar to the ones used for composite sketches of 
police suspects. It is my understanding from reading papers on Habitat 
that it did something like this as well (but more simple).

Admittedly, the result is fair to partly cheesy, but begins to add to
the information stream. Of course, people have full control over how they
are represented... but the result is nevertheless an expression of and an
extension of some aspect of the root identity.

Now intuition is effective because it interprets sight, touch, taste,
sound and smell into higher-level information without a lot of conscious
processing (he looks shifty, she feels tense, you smell sexy, etc.)
Note that the information is often conveyed unconsciously, for which 
reason our intuition gives it greater weight.

So, even if we accept the probability that we will someday be able 
to engage in the same kind of mutual information exchange that I would
get now by running my (real) fingers through your (real) hair, it
wouldn't really be the same, because, either end of the exchange could 
would still be under fully conscious control, or under control of 
a cleverly designed "silky hair" module, or "light touch" module.

Would intuition be bankrupt as a result of this? Certainly I wouldn't 
choose to look shifty, if I wanted to convince you of my good intentions, 
right? And I certainly wouldn't want my hair to be greasy or tangled,
or my fingers to be thick and rough... even with the bandwidth to
communicate these things, our belief in these "small things" revealing
truth would be sorely undermined.

So what do we do about this intuition stuff? As the the ability of the 
medium to transmit to all senses improves, I see a couple of things 
happening:

1) There will be more opportunities for manifestation of unconscious
   behavior, which can lead to the development of _new_ sources of
   intuition. For instance, I have a friend who 'talks' me before
   quitting time so we can decide whether to go for coffee. I can tell
   pretty well from meta-textual information (typing speed, response
   time) whether he's just too beat to go out (but too polite to say
   anything.)

2) As a corollary, the more bandwidth that is transmitted, the less
   fully conscious control can be put on all outputs. Patterns of
   changes to the outputs become the meta-text. (Ever feel, for 
   instance, that you were being manipulated by someone who just seemed
   to good to be real?) Of course, people will employ increasingly
   sophisticated programs to manage, say, their scent output...
   how is this constructively different from making a choice in
   perfume or aftershave? (With smelling 'natural' being another choice,
   with sub-options of 'a natural what')

[ If you think the smell stuff is outrageous, I can vouch that there is
already a demand for it... various cybersex-oriented Mushes and Mucks
have extensive 'smell messages'. ]

Note that everything that I'm outlining here from a VR point of view
should be possible from a transhuman point of view, which is really
going to throw your intuition for a loop. (For instance, I could use
a module that caused me to smile more often, and I'd certainly like
to be able to casually experiment with different appearances in 
meatspace.) 

So, if I have full control over some matter that is configured on me 
as 'hair' and you have full control over some matter that is configured
on you as 'fingers', and I have chosen my hair to be long and soft
and you have chosen your fingers to be long and soft yet strong, and
you run your fingers through my hair, how is this going to be any 
different from a (truly) full-bandwidth equivalent where I have chosen
hair and you have chosen fingers in the same configuration (albeit in
VR instead of using matter)? 

Hmmm.

Doug

