Date:     Sun, 5 Dec 93 0:10:20 GMT
From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Re:  Love and Relationship in the Transhuman Age

There are people now (check out alt.polyamory) who extend romantic love
to more than one other person at a time--I'd think that expanded
bandwidth and greater processing capacity would make larger romantic
groups possible.

I would hope that the Singularity would offer both the ability to
enjoy touch more and to need it less....in general, it's an interesting
question--would post-Singularity folk *need* input (of all sorts)
more than we do (it's hard to bore an amoeba, though I've heard
that it's possible*), or would they also have vastly increased
ability to amuse themselves alone?

If the people who are building the Singularity technology are
generally *not* touchy-feely types, will they even bother to
expand the ability to enjoy affectionate touch?

Ideally, the Net and desk-top manufacturing will free up enough
of people's time so that, if they want to have a lot of personal
contact, they'll be able to do more of it than they can now. It's
conceivable that VR will be able to convey everything that 
personal presence can, but on the other hand, it would also mean
that anything that's sent by VR can be edited, modified, or
simulated....this might make intuition even more important
than it is now.

This gets to something I've been mulling--mere biological engineering,
even without a Singularity, will make people *much* more different
from each other than they are now. At this point, people's defaults
for dominance/submission signals, comfortable group size, and such
at least are somewhat similar--but what if you have people whose
social instincts are based on wolverines, elephants, and wolves?
I'm pretty sure that people can accomodate, but it's going to
take some careful thinking and work on negotiation skills--or
possibly extraordinary abilities at liking people and communicating
with them. .

This connects to what you were saying by way of the idea that
I hope the Singularity will offer niches both to people who
want a lot of contact (perhaps even merging) and people who
want little or none.

Cheers and, if you'd like them, hugs,
Nancy

*How to bore an amoeba: I've heard that if you keep an amoeba in a
perfectly stable ideal environment, it will die. I have no idea
if this is true.
